## Context
Asked by the author (2026-10-21), from the IDH harness repo, where the collision had to be policed by a CI job
(`cross-pr-ticket-collision`: fail a pull request whose added ticket ID another open pull request also adds) that
was switched off the same day with GitHub Actions. Evidence of the pain: `docs/erg-manual.md` (ID allocation is
optimistic: two concurrent invocations in different checkouts can take the same number), the existing
post-check retry loop, the 200 ms "branch-tip scan exceeded" warning of `erg new`, and the harness's own CI
guard built for it (git-erg#282). Today two branches that each add `tickets/NNNN-slug-a.erg` and
`tickets/NNNN-slug-b.erg` have different paths, so git sees no conflict; the duplicate surfaces only when the
corpus check (`erg check`) runs on the merged tree.

What the claim adds over the existing checks: the pre-commit hook does not run on a rebase or a forge-side
merge, CI checks a merge ref that goes stale when a sibling lands, and the cross-PR job compares open pull
requests only with each other. An add/add conflict needs none of them: the forge recomputes mergeability
when the base moves, a local rebase stops, and it works offline and on any forge.

## Evidence (2026-11-08 survey of the 16 ticket stores on padme)
Method: git rename detection for ticket files whose number changed (collisions caught and renumbered),
and `ls-tree` of every first-parent default-branch commit for two ticket files sharing a number
(collisions that reached the default branch). Positive control: a duplicate made in a scratch repo was
found. Duplicate counts are an upper bound: arena's one hit (1048, 2026-10-15) is a slug rename that left
the old file behind for one commit, not a collision.

| Repo | Renumbers (last) | Duplicate IDs on the default branch (last) |
|---|---|---|
| AEDIST-technical-report | 54 (2026-06-16) | 34 (2026-06-16) |
| climate-finance-het | 29 (2026-10-23) | 6 (2026-07-27) |
| IDH harness | 12 (2026-10-01) | 0 |
| search-works-for-zotero | 6 (2026-09-09) | 5 (2026-09-09) |
| chemin-de-voix | 1 (2026-04-29) | 6 (2026-05-17) |
| padme | 1 (2026-08-21, a migrate) | 1 (2026-06-09) |
| sen-causal-reading | 1 (2026-11-06) | 0 |
| archiveCIRED | 1 (2026-06-24) | 0 |
| git-erg, arena, DOIfetch, fuzzy-corpus, cadens, JETP-observer | 0 | 0 |

Reading: collisions reached a default branch about 50 times from April to 2026-09-09 (commit messages
confirm "main was red on a duplicate ID" in search-works-for-zotero and climate-finance-het), and none
since. They still happen: two since the harness's CI job was switched off on 2026-10-21
(climate-finance-het 2650->2700 on 2026-10-23, sen-causal-reading 0099->0102 on 2026-11-06), both caught
before merge. So the current gates detect; what remains is the renumber cost, which the claim does not
reduce, and the gates' dependence on CI and agent discipline, which the claim removes. Why duplicates
stopped reaching main after September is not established.

## Design
Amended 2026-11-08 after a review of the author's design (2026-10-21); the changes are marked (A).

- `erg new NNNN` also writes `tickets/.claims/NNNN`, a file of exactly one line: the ticket's slug, a
  space, and a random nonce (8 hex digits, `crypto/rand`). (A: the slug alone is not enough, see below.)
- Two branches claiming the same number create the same path with different content: git reports an add/add
  conflict when the second one is merged or rebased. Tested 2026-11-08 in a scratch repo: merge and rebase
  conflict on the claim; a different number merges clean.
- Two claimants with the same number and the same slug (two sessions filing the same incident) do not
  always share a ticket path: one may already be closed on its branch (`tickets/closed/`) while the other
  is open. With slug-only claims, both claim files and both ticket paths then differ or agree in a way git
  merges clean. (A, from the 2026-11-08 Blind-Spot pass: the earlier text relied on the ticket files
  conflicting.) The nonce makes every claim unique, so every same-number pair conflicts on the claim.
- Claims are never deleted, neither by `erg close` nor by `erg rm`: the directory is the record of numbers
  ever used. A claim without a ticket is therefore legitimate, not an error. (A: the earlier text both kept
  claims forever and reported a claim without a ticket.)
- Allocation stays dense: `erg new` takes one above the highest number among the tickets and the claims
  (base, sibling worktrees, branch tips, as today). (A: holes are never reused. The lowest hole is as
  contended as the frontier, and a hole is usually a number an abandoned branch used, still cited by
  branch names, pull request titles and commit messages; reusing it makes `git log --grep NNNN` ambiguous.)
- Recovery: the later claimant renumbers to a number well clear of the high-water mark, per
  `erg integration`, not to the next free one. (A: the earlier text said "next free number", the most
  contended seat, which `erg integration` documents as the cause of a three-step collision chain.) The
  rename covers the claim, the ticket file, its log, references from other tickets on the branch
  (`Blocked-by:`, body), code comments and config that cite the number (climate-finance-het's
  2650->2700 renumber edited two code files), the branch name, and the pull request's title and close
  claim: a stale "Closes NNNN" would close the winner's ticket at merge.
- A slug rename must rewrite the claim too; a rename that leaves the old file behind (arena, 2026-10-15)
  then fails the slug-mismatch check below.

### Backstops (A: required, not optional)
The conflict can be silenced: a merge with `-X ours`, a conflict resolved by keeping one side, or a
`merge=union` attribute on the claims all merge clean (tested 2026-11-08: `-X ours` lands both
`NNNN-*.erg` files under one claim; union writes a two-line claim). `erg check` must therefore reject:
- a claim that is not exactly one line;
- a claim whose first field does not match its ticket file's slug (the nonce is not checked: a hand-made
  claim may carry any second field);
- a ticket without a claim (after migration), with an error that prints the plain-file fix
  (`echo "SLUG $(date +%s)" > tickets/.claims/NNNN`), so a hand-made ticket still needs no binary (Agnostic invariant);
- the existing duplicate-ID check stays.

### Staging (A)
`erg new` does not commit, and agents add the ticket by name (`git add tickets/NNNN-*` misses
`tickets/.claims/NNNN`, tested). The claim then sits untracked: `erg check` passes locally because the file
is on disk, and only a fresh clone in CI fails. So:
- `erg new` prints the `git add` line naming both files;
- the pre-commit hook rejects a staged ticket file whose claim is not in the index;
- `erg new` runs `git check-ignore` on the claim path and refuses when it is ignored (some repos, the IDH
  harness, ignore everything and allowlist paths).

### Activation and layout (A, from the 2026-11-08 Blind-Spot pass)
- Claims are on in a store exactly when its `.claims/` directory exists: a file-state switch, visible and
  reversible by hand. `erg init` creates it; the migration creates it for an existing store. `erg new` in a
  store without `.claims/` writes no claim and prints a hint to run the migration; `erg check` requires
  claims only when the directory exists. So updating the binary never fails an existing corpus.
- Mixed versions after activation: an old binary (an outstanding branch, a native macOS or Windows binary
  that `erg sync` does not replace) creates a claimless ticket; `erg check` rejects it with the plain-file
  fix. The harness retires its collision gate only once its store is migrated.
- Claims live in `<store>/.claims/`, the store `erg new` resolved (auto-discovered or `DIR`). The numbering
  space is that store, as `next-id` already scans it (recursively, `closed/` included). A store nested
  inside another store is unsupported for claims; the survey found none (all 16 stores on padme hold only
  `tickets/` and `tickets/closed/`). `next-id` counts claim numbers too, so a removed ticket's number stays
  burned.

### Migration
The migration step (`erg migrate`, or its successor) writes `tickets/.claims/NNNN` (slug and nonce) for every
existing ticket, open and closed, so the holes are real holes from the first day. It is idempotent. (A) When
two tickets already share a number, it writes no claim for that number, reports both files and exits
non-zero: one claim cannot hold two slugs. Re-running it never rewrites an existing claim.

### Docs (A)
`erg integration` says "No reservation machinery exists or is planned": reword it to describe the claims.

### Retires (A: the condition the author set for building it, 2026-11-08)
In git-erg, in this ticket:
- `erg new`'s post-check retry loop (O_EXCL on the ticket path, glob `NNNN-*.erg`, delete and retry, up to
  20 attempts; about 55 lines in `src/go/new.go`). The claim path depends on the number alone, so an
  O_EXCL create of `tickets/.claims/NNNN` fails on exactly the collision the glob detects, without the
  delete-and-retry race. Retired in stores with claims only; stores without `.claims/` keep it
  (see Plan, Decisions).
- Three manual disciplines in `erg integration`: re-running the collision scan at the merge gate, the
  `gh pr view` loop over open pull requests, and "run `erg check` against `origin/main` after a merge,
  the only collision check in a repo with no CI". The add/add conflict covers the base tip and the
  sibling pull request (at the second merge, before any duplicate lands), with no forge call. The
  renumber-clear-of-the-frontier rule stays.
In the IDH harness, by a ticket filed there once a release carries the claims:
- `scripts/check-cross-pr-ticket-collision.sh` (173 lines), its test (450 lines),
  `.github/workflows/ticket-collision.yml`, the `scripts/local-ci.sh` wiring and its `tickets/LOCAL.md`
  line: a forge-dependent gate (gh auth, network, self-PR ambiguity; harness ticket 1325) already
  switched off.
Gained where nothing ran: search-works-for-zotero has no CI, and five duplicates reached its main.
Kept: the sibling-worktree and branch-tip scans of `next-id`. They lower how often collisions happen;
the claim only detects them. Without the scans every parallel session would meet a rebase conflict and
a renumber. The duplicate-ID check of `erg check` stays as the backstop.

### Not in this ticket
Detection happens when the second claimant reaches the base, not at allocation. The stronger option for a
central origin (push an atomic ref `refs/erg/id/NNNN`, git-erg#282) needs the network.

Per `AGENTS.md` § Earning a feature the burden is on adding: if collisions stop biting, the ticket can be
closed unbuilt.

## Plan (raid 797d07, 2026-11-08; Plan seat opus, decisions checked by an advisor, fable)
Feasibility: PASS (anchors verified: new.go:236 createExclusive, archive.go:39, atomicwrite.go:54,
migrate.go:91, check.go:250, AGENTS.md 2393/2400 bytes, test_selfcoherence.sh:106 hook mirror,
ADOPTER_STRINGS in test_integration.sh:125, Makefile TEST_SUITES and docs/regen-assets).

Decisions:
- Activation: `erg migrate --claims` (a flag on the existing verb; bare `erg migrate` stays the remedy
  other warnings name and must not switch claims on). `erg init` creates `.claims/` only in an empty
  store. Both write `.claims/.keep` (git does not carry an empty directory); dot names are ignored by
  check and next-id.
- Claim write: temp (dot-prefixed, in `.claims/`) then `os.Link` to `.claims/NNNN` (EEXIST is the
  collision signal; no clobber, never a partial claim). A claim orphaned by a kill before the ticket is
  written only burns a number, like a removed ticket's claim: not reported.
- The glob post-check retry loop is retired in stores with claims and kept for stores without, because
  10 concurrent `erg new` in one claims-off checkout all took one number (measured by gaze and the
  worker on PR #386), a regression for every unmigrated store. (Revised by the orchestrator after
  that measurement; the first version retired it fully.) The N=10 race test runs on both kinds of store.
- `erg new` stdout stays `CREATED <path>` alone (about 47 test sites parse it); the `git add` line and
  the migrate hint go to stderr.
- `spec-erg-v1.md` "Collision handling" documents claims: the plain-file contract changes.
- git-erg's own store is not migrated in this PR (the committed bootstrap binary is rebuilt only after
  merge); adoption per repo is the author's call.

Actions: new `src/go/claims.go` (claimsDir with Lstat symlink refusal, claimsActive, parseClaimName,
newNonce, claimIgnored via `git check-ignore`, writeClaim, claimViolations, migrateClaims); `new.go`
cmdNew loop replaced, test seam `allocateID`; `nextid.go` maxIDInDir and maxIDInRef count claim names;
`check.go` claimViolations beside assetViolations; help texts of rm/close/archive say the claim is kept;
`init.go`; `migrate.go` flag parser and `--claims`, `helptext.go` Args; `install.go` hookBody block
(POSIX sh) mirrored byte for byte in `integration.md` section 1; `integration.md` section 5 rewritten
(the three manual scans and "No reservation machinery" removed, rename scope added, no adopter names);
`src/go/assets/AGENTS.md` within its 2400-byte ceiling; `make regen-assets`, `make docs`.
Tests: first red in new_test.go (claim content `^slug [0-9a-f]{8}$`); new suite `tests/test_claims.sh`
in TEST_SUITES (bare origin plus two clones; merge and rebase conflict; negative control; same slug with
one closed; `-X ours` and `merge=union` caught by the specific check messages); allocation, check-rule
table, hook, migration idempotence (byte-identical), seam race test; test_integration.sh assertions
updated. Measure `build/erg` size and `make test-scaling` before and after.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] `erg new` writes `tickets/.claims/NNNN` (one line: slug and nonce) when `.claims/` exists, prints the `git add` line for both
      files, and refuses an ignored claim path; `erg close` and `erg rm` leave the claim in place.
- [ ] A test with two branches adding the same number with different slugs: merge and rebase report an
      add/add conflict on the claim file (positive control); claims with different numbers merge clean
      (negative control); same number and same slug with one copy closed also conflicts.
- [ ] Without `.claims/`, `erg new` writes no claim and `erg check` requires none (an existing corpus
      passes after a binary update).
- [ ] `erg new` allocates above the highest ticket or claim number and never reuses a hole.
- [ ] `erg check` rejects a multi-line claim, a slug mismatch, and a ticket without a claim (after
      migration, with the plain-file fix in the message); a claim without a ticket passes. Tests cover the
      `-X ours` and `merge=union` silencings.
- [ ] The pre-commit hook rejects a staged ticket whose claim is not staged.
- [ ] The migration populates the claims for all existing tickets (open and closed), is idempotent, and
      refuses a number two tickets share.
- [ ] `erg new`'s glob post-check retry loop is replaced by an O_EXCL create of the claim; a test races
      two creations of the same number in one checkout.
- [ ] The manual's ID-allocation section, `erg integration` (recovery: rename scope, claims; the three
      manual collision scans removed) and `tickets/AGENTS.md` describe the claims and the rename step.
- [ ] A ticket filed in the IDH harness to retire its cross-PR collision gate once a git-erg release
      carries the claims and the harness store is migrated.
- [ ] `make check` passes; the bootstrap binary is rebuilt by the normal route.
